2. The scientific illustration library is NOT distributed here
================================================================================

The editor ships with a library of roughly 24,000 illustrations when built as an
application, but that library is NOT part of this repository. `.gitignore`
excludes:

    data/              the imported catalogue, assets and thumbnails
    vendor/            upstream checkouts such as Bioicons
    build/             packaged application output

Users obtain the illustrations themselves by running the import and acquisition
scripts, which download from each provider directly. Every asset therefore
reaches the user under that provider's own licence, and the catalogue records
the licence, author and source URL for each one.

That matters because the library is a mixture of licences, including some that
are not compatible with a permissive repository licence:

    CC0 / Public Domain       ~13,300
    CC-BY (3.0 / 4.0)          ~9,500
    CC-BY-SA (3.0 / 4.0)       ~1,250   share-alike obligations on adaptations
    CC-BY-NC, CC-BY-NC-SA        ~750   non-commercial use only

The non-commercial and share-alike assets come almost entirely from PhyloPic.

Providers and their terms:

  * NIH BIOART Source — U.S. National Institutes of Health, NIAID Visual &
    Medical Arts. Public domain; NIH asks that use does not imply endorsement.
    https://bioart.niaid.nih.gov/
  * Bioicons — https://bioicons.com/ — per-icon licences (CC0, CC-BY-3.0,
    CC-BY-4.0, CC-BY-SA, MIT, BSD-3-Clause). Includes Servier Medical Art
    (CC-BY-3.0) and DBCLS illustrations (CC-BY-4.0).
  * Reactome — https://reactome.org/ — CC-BY-4.0.
  * Togo Picture Gallery — DBCLS, https://togotv.dbcls.jp/ — CC-BY-4.0.
  * SciDraw — https://scidraw.io/ — CC-BY-4.0 or CC0 per illustration.
  * Health Icons — https://healthicons.org/ — CC0.
  * SciSVG — CC-BY-4.0.
  * PhyloPic — https://www.phylopic.org/ — mixed, including CC-BY-SA and
    CC-BY-NC. Check the licence of any silhouette before commercial use.

Full licence texts for the Bioicons families are kept in
`reports/bioicons/licenses/`.

================================================================================
3. Images committed in this repository
================================================================================

Images are committed under `docs/images/`, `examples/` and `reports/`.

The screenshots and figures used in README.md carry these third-party
illustrations, all redistributed under the licences shown:

  docs/images/figure-workflow.png
      Person, by Austin J. Athman — NIH BIOART Source — public domain
      Syringe, by Ryan Kissinger — NIH BIOART Source — public domain
  docs/images/figure-panels.png
      Lab Mouse, by Ryan Kissinger — NIH BIOART Source — public domain
      liver, by Servier — Bioicons — CC-BY-3.0
  docs/images/editor.png
      A screenshot of the editor displaying the Bioicons import verification
      document; each illustration in it is labelled on screen with its author
      and licence (Servier CC-BY-3.0, DBCLS CC-BY-4.0, and others).

docs/images/figure-crispr.png, docs/images/figure-pathway.png and
docs/images/glyphs.png contain no third-party illustrations: every element in
them is drawn by the procedural engine in this repository.

Further example and verification images are committed under `examples/` and
`reports/`. Those that were composed from library illustrations
carry their credits printed in the figure itself; for example
`examples/aavs1-knockin-compose_layout.png` credits a CRISPR-Cas9 illustration
by DBCLS (Bioicons, CC-BY-4.0) and an iPSC illustration from NIH BIOART Source
(public domain).

The SVG files committed under `examples/` and `samples/` contain no third-party
illustrations: they are original artwork and original importer fixtures.

================================================================================
4. Attribution produced by the tool
================================================================================

When a figure uses library illustrations, `generate_figure` writes a
`<name>.credits.txt` file beside the exported SVG and PNG listing each
illustration with its author, provider and licence, and the exported SVG embeds
the same information as metadata. Keep that attribution with any figure you
publish; CC-BY and CC-BY-SA require it.
